WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.726 --> 00:00:04.212
- Early on, a writer for
one of the traditional

00:00:04.212 --> 00:00:06.488
whiskey magazines wrote that our product

00:00:06.488 --> 00:00:08.400
tasted like sheep piss.

00:00:08.400 --> 00:00:09.806
Like sheep piss.

00:00:09.806 --> 00:00:12.337
I mean, that was so
ridiculous and so absurd.

00:00:12.337 --> 00:00:14.843
People said, "How would somebody know

00:00:14.843 --> 00:00:16.875
what sheep piss tastes like?"

00:00:16.875 --> 00:00:19.035
And if, in fact, that's
what he's drinking,

00:00:19.035 --> 00:00:22.673
he should be disqualified from
ever judging whiskey again.

00:00:22.673 --> 00:00:25.923
(lively strings music)

00:00:27.558 --> 00:00:30.159
There's a whiskey shortage
in the world right now.

00:00:30.159 --> 00:00:33.140
Not everybody realizes it,
but prices are going up.

00:00:33.140 --> 00:00:35.323
Some bottles are
disappearing from the shelves

00:00:35.323 --> 00:00:37.866
because people can't keep up with demand.

00:00:37.866 --> 00:00:39.950
Typically, you make a distilled spirit

00:00:39.950 --> 00:00:41.158
and you put it in a barrel,

00:00:41.158 --> 00:00:44.424
and you wait for eight or 10 or 12 years,

00:00:44.424 --> 00:00:46.505
and I'm not that patient a person.

00:00:46.505 --> 00:00:50.794
Generally, the stuff that we
make only takes about 24 hours.

00:00:50.794 --> 00:00:52.675
We're not a craft distiller.

00:00:52.675 --> 00:00:54.063
We're not a micro-distiller.

00:00:54.063 --> 00:00:56.205
We are a technology company that works

00:00:56.205 --> 00:00:58.054
in the whiskey space.

00:00:58.054 --> 00:01:02.115
80% of the flavor in a
whiskey comes from the wood.

00:01:02.115 --> 00:01:03.638
There are some amazing woods out there

00:01:03.638 --> 00:01:05.222
that we couldn't build barrels from,

00:01:05.222 --> 00:01:07.016
because they would leak like a sieve.

00:01:07.016 --> 00:01:10.455
We take the clear spirit
and, along with the wood,

00:01:10.455 --> 00:01:13.147
we put them in pressure-capable
stainless steel tanks,

00:01:13.147 --> 00:01:14.932
squeezing the wood like a sponge,

00:01:14.932 --> 00:01:17.262
moving the alcohol in and
out of the porous structure

00:01:17.262 --> 00:01:18.262
of the wood.

00:01:19.695 --> 00:01:22.270
The industry thinks that's heresy.

00:01:22.270 --> 00:01:24.005
What we do is sacrilege.

00:01:24.005 --> 00:01:26.561
They think it's awful,
just like people thought

00:01:26.561 --> 00:01:29.554
the internal combustion engine was awful,

00:01:29.554 --> 00:01:32.530
and the only way to get from
point A to point B faster

00:01:32.530 --> 00:01:34.628
was to breed faster horses.

00:01:34.628 --> 00:01:36.471
Well, that has obviously changed,

00:01:36.471 --> 00:01:40.129
and we're changing the very
nature of the whiskey industry.

00:01:40.129 --> 00:01:41.694
- [Interviewer] Why even call it whiskey?

00:01:41.694 --> 00:01:44.234
Why wouldn't you call it
spirits or something else?

00:01:44.234 --> 00:01:46.106
- Technically, it meets
all the requirements

00:01:46.106 --> 00:01:47.614
of a whiskey or a bourbon

00:01:47.614 --> 00:01:49.198
or whatever we happen to be making.

00:01:49.198 --> 00:01:50.647
If we called it something else,

00:01:50.647 --> 00:01:53.549
retailers wouldn't know
what to do with it.

00:01:53.549 --> 00:01:56.450
Why should it matter how we make it?

00:01:56.450 --> 00:01:58.618
What should matter is the taste.

00:01:58.618 --> 00:02:00.414
I've always been sort of a trouble-maker.

00:02:00.414 --> 00:02:01.996
I've always been a rule-breaker.

00:02:01.996 --> 00:02:04.076
It's like, “Wow, we did something,

00:02:04.076 --> 00:02:06.897
and nobody ever tried this before.”

00:02:06.897 --> 00:02:08.610
That's pretty cool.

00:02:08.610 --> 00:02:11.443
(inspiring music)

